{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>before his father Terah, in the land of his birth</b> — Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 11:28), following the way of our Rabbis (Bereshit Rabbah 38:13), that Terah denounced his son Abram before Nimrod for breaking his idols, and he cast him into the fiery furnace. Haran sat and said: if Abram prevails, I am his, and if Nimrod prevails, I am his. When Abram was saved, they said to him: whose are you? He said to them: I am Abram's. They cast him into the furnace and he was burned, and this is Ur Kasdim.\n\nAnd Menachem explained אוּר as 'valley' — as in 'therefore בָּאֻרִים glorify YHWH' (Isaiah 24:15), and similarly 'מְאוּרַת the viper' (Isaiah 11:8): every hole and deep valley is called אוּר.\n\nAnd the matter that our Rabbis received regarding this is the truth, and I shall explain it. Our father Abraham was not born in the land of the Chaldees, for his ancestors were of the sons of Shem, while the Chaldees and all the land of Shinar are the lands of the sons of Ham. And Scripture says, 'and they told Abram הָעִבְרִי' (Genesis 14:13) — not 'the Chaldean.' And it is written, 'beyond the River your fathers dwelt מֵעוֹלָם — Terah the father of Abraham and the father of Nahor' (Joshua 24:2), and the word מֵעוֹלָם teaches that his lineage was from there of old. And it is written, 'and I took your father Abraham מֵעֵבֶר הַנָּהָר' (Joshua 24:3).\n\nAnd a proof for this: Nahor was in Haran. Now if Terah's place had been Ur Kasdim in the land of Shinar, and Scripture recounted that when he went out from Ur Kasdim he took with him only Abram his son and Lot his grandson and Sarai his daughter-in-law — if so, Nahor would have remained in the land of the Chaldees. But in truth their birthplace is the land of Aram beyond the River, and it was the ancestral heritage from of old. And Scripture says of the sons of Shem, 'and their dwelling was from Mesha as you go toward Sephar, the mountain of the east' (Genesis 10:30), and this is an inclusive term for Shem, as it is written, 'in their lands, after their nations' (Genesis 10:31). And it is written, 'from Aram Balak king of Moab brought me, from the mountains of the east' (Numbers 23:7) — so he and his ancestors were from that land from of old.\n\nAnd we found in the Talmud (Bava Batra 91a) that Abraham was imprisoned in Cutha, and this city is not in the land of the Chaldees, as it is written, 'and the king of Assyria brought men from Babylon and from Cutha and from Avva and from Hamath' (II Kings 17:24), and it is written, 'and the men of Babylon made Succoth-benoth, and the men of Cuth made Nergal' (II Kings 17:30). Rather, it appears that it is a city beyond the River in the land of Aram Naharaim, for Haran is the name of a city in the land of Aram Naharaim, as it is written, 'and he went to Aram Naharaim, to the city of Nahor' (Genesis 24:10), which is Haran.\n\nAnd furthermore we investigated and learned from many scholars who were inhabitants of that land, that Cutha is a great city between Haran and Assyria, far from the province of Babylon, and between it and Haran is about six days' journey. But it is included in 'beyond the River' because it lies between Aram Naharaim and the Euphrates, the border of the land of Israel, and the Hidakel which goes east of Assyria.\n\nSo Terah begot his elder sons Abraham and Nahor beyond the River, in the land of his ancestors, and he journeyed with his son Abraham to the land of the Chaldees, and there his younger son Haran was born to him, while his son Nahor remained beyond the River in the city of Haran — either he was born in that city or he settled there from Cutha. And this is the meaning of 'in the land of his birth, in Ur Kasdim' — for that was the birthplace of Haran alone.\n\nAnd this received tradition is also found in the book of ancient lore of the nations, as the Rav wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:29), for they mentioned in the book 'The Agricultural Work of the Egyptians' that Abram, who was born in Cutha, disputed the opinion of the multitudes who worshipped the sun, and the king placed him in prison, and he remained with them in confinement there for many days. Afterward the king feared that he would bring destruction upon his land and turn people away from their faith, and he banished him to the far end of the land of Canaan after seizing all his wealth.\n\nSo in any case, in that place, in the land of the Chaldees, a miracle was performed for our father Abraham — either a hidden miracle, that He placed in the heart of that king to save him and not put him to death and to bring him out of prison so he could go free, or an open miracle, that they cast him into the fiery furnace and he was delivered, according to the words of our Rabbis.\n\nAnd do not let Rabbi Abraham seduce you with his objections, when he says that Scripture did not recount this wonder, for I shall yet give you a reason and proof regarding this and similar matters (Ramban on Genesis 46:15). But those nations did not mention this in their book, because they disputed his opinion and regarded his miracle as an act of sorcery, like the case of Moses our master with the Egyptians at the beginning of his deeds. And for this reason Scripture did not further mention this miracle, for it would have been necessary to mention the words of those who disputed him, as it mentioned the words of the magicians of Egypt — and the words of Abraham with them would not have been clarified as the words of Moses our master were clarified at the end.\n\nAnd this is what Scripture says: 'I am YHWH who brought you out הוֹצֵאתִיךָ from Ur Kasdim, to give you this land to inherit it' (Genesis 15:7). For the word הוֹצֵאתִיךָ teaches about a miracle, for it did not say 'who took you (לְקַחְתִּיךָ) from Ur Kasdim,' but said הוֹצֵאתִיךָ — bringing out from the confinement of a prisoner, as in 'who brought you out הוֹצֵאתִיךָ from the land of Egypt' (Exodus 20:2). And it says 'to give you this land to inherit it,' for from the time He brought him out of Ur Kasdim it was the will before Him, may He be exalted, to make him great and to give him that land.\n\nAnd his father Terah and Abraham — from that day when he was saved, it was in their hearts to go to the land of Canaan, to distance themselves from the land of the Chaldees out of fear of the king. For Haran was near them, and they were one people and one language to all of them, for Aramaic was the tongue of both. And they wanted to go to a people whose language that king and his people would not understand, and this is the meaning of 'and they went out with them from Ur Kasdim to go to the land of Canaan, and they came as far as Haran' (Genesis 11:31) — where their families and ancestors had been from of old, and they settled among them and tarried there many days.\n\nAnd there Abraham was commanded to do what had arisen in his mind — to go to the land of Canaan. He left his father, and his father died there in Haran, his land, while he went with his wife and Lot his brother's son to the land of Canaan. And this is what Scripture says: 'and I took your father Abraham from beyond the River, and I led him through all the land of Canaan' (Joshua 24:3) — for beyond the River he was commanded concerning this, and from there He took him and led him through all the land of Canaan.\n\nNow according to the opinion of our Rabbis, Ur Kasdim is taken literally, from the root 'I warmed myself (חַמּוֹתִי) — I saw אוּר' (Isaiah 44:16), and they said <b>and they went out with them from Ur Kasdim</b> — and Terah did not emerge from the furnace, but Abram is the principal one. Or else the place was so named on account of the miracle, as in 'and in Taberah and in Massah and in Kivrot Hata'avah' (Deuteronomy 9:22) and others like them, and Scripture hints that when Abram emerged from the furnace they all fled from there.\n\nAnd the interpretation of 'בָּאֻרִים glorify YHWH' (Isaiah 24:15), in my opinion, follows their words — for these are the high mountains where they kindle lights and raise signal fires to broadcast news swiftly to distant places, as it says there, 'in the islands of the sea, the name of YHWH, Elohim of Israel' (Isaiah 24:15). The meaning is that they make known throughout the entire world the miracle and wonder performed for them, for the glory of the Name. And likewise 'מְאוּרַת the viper' (Isaiah 11:8) — its hole, where its light and great heat are, as he calls it 'fiery serpent (שָׂרָף).'\n\nAnd I saw in the Midrash 'Arise, shine (קוּמִי אוֹרִי)' (Pesikta deRav Kahana, chapter 21): 'therefore בָּאֻרִים glorify YHWH' — with what do they glorify Him? Rabbi Abba bar Kahana said: with lantern-trees. These are like the lamps that are kindled in synagogues in every place, even in the islands of the sea, for the glory of the Name. See that they make 'בָּאֻרִים' from the word for fire, literally."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase עבודת האיכרים המצרים refers to a work cited by Maimonides in Guide III:29; the Hebrew title as given here is translated literally. The phrase 'חמותי ראיתי אור' (Isaiah 44:16) literally means 'I was warm, I have seen fire'; the root ח-מ-מ is rendered 'warm' to preserve the parallel Ramban draws between warmth and light/fire (אור)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:28:39.636039101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}